the singular subject \"Alexander&rsquo;s use.\"</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-644-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"938a64bc-fd46-4f1a-9b56-ed333b5bcdea","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959340,"updateDate":1691007959340}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959340, 1691007959340),
    ($SATQ$c88ba1b7$SATQ$, $SATQ$603b8a74-0a7b-4e5d-9456-729dc5f9c391$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries, automobiles were commonly referred to as horseless carriages after the older technology they still resembled. Known as the Brass Era, this period in automotive design is remembered for its grandeur and artistry, its vehicles <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> by collectors for their ornate detailing and gleaming brass fittings.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"d9c87091-4818-4692-8c20-ebc72cb2cdb8","content":"<p>are highly prized</p>"},{"id":"2aad20b4-76a6-41c3-86b1-cc89389549d0","content":"<p>had been highly prized</p>"},{"id":"b9e0184e-0ca2-4ce2-a558-34adf216c006","content":"<p>highly prized</p>"},{"id":"6493536c-09d5-4e89-adeb-1c22075da008","content":"<p>were highly prized</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["b9e0184e-0ca2-4ce2-a558-34adf216c006"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of verb forms in a sentence. The nonfinite past participle phrase "highly prized" is correctly used to form a supplementary element that modifies the main clause "this&hellip;artistry," describing memorable features of Brass Era automotive design.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. Using the finite present tense verb phrase "are highly prized" creates a second main clause in the sentence, and two main clauses can&rsquo;t be joined in this way with only a comma after "artistry." Choice B is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. Using the finite past perfect tense verb phrase "had been highly prized" creates a second main clause in the sentence, and two main clauses can&rsquo;t be joined in this way with only a comma after "artistry." Choice D is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. Using the finite past tense verb phrase "were highly prized" creates a second main clause in the sentence, and two main clauses can&rsquo;t be joined in this way with only a comma after "artistry."</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"c88ba1b7","external_id":"603b8a74-0a7b-4e5d-9456-729dc5f9c391","vaultid":null,"uId":"9f8156a0-9bf1-4832-bceb-e99334d73ebc","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries, automobiles were commonly referred to as horseless carriages after the older technology they still resembled. Known as the Brass Era, this period in automotive design is remembered for its grandeur and artistry, its vehicles <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> by collectors for their ornate detailing and gleaming brass fittings.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"d9c87091-4818-4692-8c20-ebc72cb2cdb8","content":"<p>are highly prized</p>"},{"id":"2aad20b4-76a6-41c3-86b1-cc89389549d0","content":"<p>had been highly prized</p>"},{"id":"b9e0184e-0ca2-4ce2-a558-34adf216c006","content":"<p>highly prized</p>"},{"id":"6493536c-09d5-4e89-adeb-1c22075da008","content":"<p>were highly prized</p>"}],"keys":["b9e0184e-0ca2-4ce2-a558-34adf216c006"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of verb forms in a sentence. The nonfinite past participle phrase \"highly prized\" is correctly used to form a supplementary element that modifies the main clause \"this&hellip;artistry,\" describing memorable features of Brass Era automotive design.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. Using the finite present tense verb phrase \"are highly prized\" creates a second main clause in the sentence, and two main clauses can&rsquo;t be joined in this way with only a comma after \"artistry.\" Choice B is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. Using the finite past perfect tense verb phrase \"had been highly prized\" creates a second main clause in the sentence, and two main clauses can&rsquo;t be joined in this way with only a comma after \"artistry.\" Choice D is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. Using the finite past tense verb phrase \"were highly prized\" creates a second main clause in the sentence, and two main clauses can&rsquo;t be joined in this way with only a comma after \"artistry.\"</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669208,"updateDate":1743430669208}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669208, 1743430669208),
    ($SATQ$c91ef0f0$SATQ$, $SATQ$2b30643e-3d33-441c-afc0-d5c81606dcd3$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$83d3740d-7e4b-4cfc-84c5-8e0a882e55ee$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>During the American Civil War, Thomas Morris Chester braved the front lines as a war correspondent for the <em>Philadelphia Press</em>. Amplifying the voices and experiences of Black soldiers <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> of particular importance to Chester, who later became an activist and lawyer during the postwar Reconstruction period.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"15feae6d-5e5c-4bba-94fb-8025e819bc20","content":"<p>were</p>"},{"id":"79f241b8-c42c-4fc1-a428-8d08efdf33b3","content":"<p>have been</p>"},{"id":"bf8c83b6-1284-43c3-8721-4e0a5fe9190e","content":"<p>are</p>"},{"id":"0f68a668-0a85-4c2b-83fc-144e1a42bd76","content":"<p>was</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["0f68a668-0a85-4c2b-83fc-144e1a42bd76"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-verb agreement. The singular verb "was" agrees in number with the singular subject "amplifying." Gerunds such as "amplifying" are always singular.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the plural verb "were" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject "amplifying." Choice B is incorrect because the plural verb "have been" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject "amplifying." Choice C is incorrect because the plural verb "are" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject "amplifying."</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-767-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$6a92d55c-b7f6-4108-9251-0cc3bf0f5f95$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"c91ef0f0","external_id":"2b30643e-3d33-441c-afc0-d5c81606dcd3","vaultid":"83d3740d-7e4b-4cfc-84c5-8e0a882e55ee","uId":"17682d55-ddf4-4325-8261-35f9249bad46","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>During the American Civil War, Thomas Morris Chester braved the front lines as a war correspondent for the <em>Philadelphia Press</em>. Amplifying the voices and experiences of Black soldiers <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> of particular importance to Chester, who later became an activist and lawyer during the postwar Reconstruction period.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"15feae6d-5e5c-4bba-94fb-8025e819bc20","content":"<p>were</p>"},{"id":"79f241b8-c42c-4fc1-a428-8d08efdf33b3","content":"<p>have been</p>"},{"id":"bf8c83b6-1284-43c3-8721-4e0a5fe9190e","content":"<p>are</p>"},{"id":"0f68a668-0a85-4c2b-83fc-144e1a42bd76","content":"<p>was</p>"}],"keys":["0f68a668-0a85-4c2b-83fc-144e1a42bd76"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-verb agreement. The singular verb \"was\" agrees in number with the singular subject \"amplifying.\" Gerunds such as \"amplifying\" are always singular.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the plural verb \"were\" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject \"amplifying.\" Choice B is incorrect because the plural verb \"have been\" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject \"amplifying.\" Choice C is incorrect because the plural verb \"are\" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject \"amplifying.\"</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-767-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"6a92d55c-b7f6-4108-9251-0cc3bf0f5f95","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959351,"updateDate":1691007959351}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959351, 1691007959351),
    ($SATQ$cabe71d4$SATQ$, $SATQ$f0236e18-52a2-4da5-893f-8f4c5799f85d$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$b98cf7c6-1b5a-4b4b-b493-069321539b14$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Both Sona Charaipotra, an Indian American, and Dhonielle Clayton, an African American, grew up frustrated by the lack of diverse characters in books for young people. In 2011, these two writers joined forces to found CAKE Literary, a book packaging <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> specializes in the creation and promotion of stories told from diverse perspectives for children and young adults.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"4b4ee4cf-cd03-454d-a4fa-0f98ba17ab8f","content":"<p>company,</p>"},{"id":"2117b752-de3f-4d25-b0a0-e493531f8975","content":"<p>company that</p>"},{"id":"9a13227e-0940-4956-acef-2e7dae645283","content":"<p>company</p>"},{"id":"d4141628-e363-48a6-8968-443a3eb8b08f","content":"<p>company, that</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["2117b752-de3f-4d25-b0a0-e493531f8975"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use and punctuation of an integrated relative clause. This choice correctly uses the relative pronoun &ldquo;that&rdquo; and no punctuation to create an integrated relative clause that provides essential information about the noun phrase (&ldquo;a book packaging company&rdquo;) that it modifies. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it doesn&rsquo;t use a relative pronoun to link the verb phrase beginning with &ldquo;specializes&rdquo; to the noun phrase that it modifies (&ldquo;a book packaging company&rdquo;). Choice C is incorrect because it doesn&rsquo;t use a relative pronoun to link the verb phrase beginning with &ldquo;specializes&rdquo; to the noun phrase that it modifies (&ldquo;a book packaging company&rdquo;). Choice D is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the integrated relative clause beginning with &ldquo;that specializes&rdquo; and the noun phrase that it modifies (&ldquo;a book packaging company&rdquo;). </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-618-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$75c05964-2479-45af-9615-5d17b05fde78$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"cabe71d4","external_id":"f0236e18-52a2-4da5-893f-8f4c5799f85d","vaultid":"b98cf7c6-1b5a-4b4b-b493-069321539b14","uId":"97aa6965-bab4-414c-a31a-91e4458232f5","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Both Sona Charaipotra, an Indian American, and Dhonielle Clayton, an African American, grew up frustrated by the lack of diverse characters in books for young people. In 2011, these two writers joined forces to found CAKE Literary, a book packaging <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> specializes in the creation and promotion of stories told from diverse perspectives for children and young adults.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"4b4ee4cf-cd03-454d-a4fa-0f98ba17ab8f","content":"<p>company,</p>"},{"id":"2117b752-de3f-4d25-b0a0-e493531f8975","content":"<p>company that</p>"},{"id":"9a13227e-0940-4956-acef-2e7dae645283","content":"<p>company</p>"},{"id":"d4141628-e363-48a6-8968-443a3eb8b08f","content":"<p>company, that</p>"}],"keys":["2117b752-de3f-4d25-b0a0-e493531f8975"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use and punctuation of an integrated relative clause. This choice correctly uses the relative pronoun &ldquo;that&rdquo; and no punctuation to create an integrated relative clause that provides essential information about the noun phrase (&ldquo;a book packaging company&rdquo;) that it modifies. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it doesn&rsquo;t use a relative pronoun to link the verb phrase beginning with &ldquo;specializes&rdquo; to the noun phrase that it modifies (&ldquo;a book packaging company&rdquo;). Choice C is incorrect because it doesn&rsquo;t use a relative pronoun to link the verb phrase beginning with &ldquo;specializes&rdquo; to the noun phrase that it modifies (&ldquo;a book packaging company&rdquo;). Choice D is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the integrated relative clause beginning with &ldquo;that specializes&rdquo; and the noun phrase that it modifies (&ldquo;a book packaging company&rdquo;). </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-618-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"75c05964-2479-45af-9615-5d17b05fde78","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959339,"updateDate":1691007959339}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959339, 1691007959339),
    ($SATQ$cd2443c0$SATQ$, $SATQ$8a266ac5-b158-488c-95a5-77d25a72d299$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$b4eb2a34-04ef-4b7e-addb-1f640f8dae86$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>A pioneer in the field of taphonomy (the study of how organic remains become fossils), <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> may be just as prevalent in the fossil record as those of thick-shelled organisms.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"fb3b709d-5802-4df5-892a-b51cb1ce90d9","content":"<p>fossils of thin-shelled organisms, Anna Behrensmeyer demonstrated in a 2005 analysis,</p>"},{"id":"6197162c-fb4d-4031-91d5-390ac0a41d40","content":"<p>Anna Behrensmeyer demonstrated in a 2005 analysis that fossils of thin-shelled organisms</p>"},{"id":"cf7e5506-0b04-445d-ba2d-2f58894171b2","content":"<p>it was demonstrated in a 2005 analysis by Anna Behrensmeyer that fossils of thin-shelled organisms</p>"},{"id":"dd6b6e59-3020-4219-aa22-7c912308ecc9","content":"<p>a 2005 analysis&mdash;by Anna Behrensmeyer&mdash;demonstrated that fossils of thin-shelled organisms</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["6197162c-fb4d-4031-91d5-390ac0a41d40"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-modifier placement. This choice makes the noun phrase &ldquo;Anna Behrensmeyer&rdquo; the subject of the sentence and places it immediately after the modifying phrase &ldquo;a pioneer&hellip;fossils.&rdquo; In doing so, this choice clearly establishes that Anna Behrensmeyer&mdash;and not another noun in the sentence&mdash;is a pioneer in the field of taphonomy. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun phrase &ldquo;fossils of thin-shelled organisms&rdquo; immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that the &ldquo;fossils&rdquo; are a pioneer in the field of taphonomy.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the pronoun &ldquo;it&rdquo; immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that &ldquo;it&rdquo; is a pioneer in the field of taphonomy. Choice D is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun phrase &ldquo;a 2005 analysis&rdquo; immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that &ldquo;a 2005 analysis&rdquo; is a pioneer in the field of taphonomy.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-742-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$e0921d43-22fd-4d22-8256-c76556f915e6$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"cd2443c0","external_id":"8a266ac5-b158-488c-95a5-77d25a72d299","vaultid":"b4eb2a34-04ef-4b7e-addb-1f640f8dae86","uId":"f49b5ecb-6cf1-4f5f-980b-0478185c279a","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>A pioneer in the field of taphonomy (the study of how organic remains become fossils), <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> may be just as prevalent in the fossil record as those of thick-shelled organisms.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"fb3b709d-5802-4df5-892a-b51cb1ce90d9","content":"<p>fossils of thin-shelled organisms, Anna Behrensmeyer demonstrated in a 2005 analysis,</p>"},{"id":"6197162c-fb4d-4031-91d5-390ac0a41d40","content":"<p>Anna Behrensmeyer demonstrated in a 2005 analysis that fossils of thin-shelled organisms</p>"},{"id":"cf7e5506-0b04-445d-ba2d-2f58894171b2","content":"<p>it was demonstrated in a 2005 analysis by Anna Behrensmeyer that fossils of thin-shelled organisms</p>"},{"id":"dd6b6e59-3020-4219-aa22-7c912308ecc9","content":"<p>a 2005 analysis&mdash;by Anna Behrensmeyer&mdash;demonstrated that fossils of thin-shelled organisms</p>"}],"keys":["6197162c-fb4d-4031-91d5-390ac0a41d40"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-modifier placement. This choice makes the noun phrase &ldquo;Anna Behrensmeyer&rdquo; the subject of the sentence and places it immediately after the modifying phrase &ldquo;a pioneer&hellip;fossils.&rdquo; In doing so, this choice clearly establishes that Anna Behrensmeyer&mdash;and not another noun in the sentence&mdash;is a pioneer in the field of taphonomy. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun phrase &ldquo;fossils of thin-shelled organisms&rdquo; immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that the &ldquo;fossils&rdquo; are a pioneer in the field of taphonomy.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the pronoun &ldquo;it&rdquo; immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that &ldquo;it&rdquo; is a pioneer in the field of taphonomy. Choice D is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun phrase &ldquo;a 2005 analysis&rdquo; immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that &ldquo;a 2005 analysis&rdquo; is a pioneer in the field of taphonomy.&nbsp;</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-742-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"e0921d43-22fd-4d22-8256-c76556f915e6","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959350,"updateDate":1691007959350}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959350, 1691007959350),
    ($SATQ$cd4b1a7e$SATQ$, $SATQ$aca54523-e3d5-4486-aac0-0b3b70e3ecbe$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>How did whales, once no bigger than seals, evolve to become the largest animals on Earth? Brazilian biologist Mariana Nery believes the answer might be found in whales&rsquo; DNA. In January 2023, Nery and her colleagues <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> a study showing changes over time in four whale genes associated with body size.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"664e7eb6-c566-45e9-affb-6ca2036f8a9a","content":"<p>published</p>"},{"id":"740e8d59-795d-4dcd-9bed-26ff4679c9c5","content":"<p>publishing</p>"},{"id":"641acfeb-420f-4320-9885-302818a1b88f","content":"<p>having published</p>"},{"id":"c917b569-b893-4649-b2d4-9d113a008396","content":"<p>to publish</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["664e7eb6-c566-45e9-affb-6ca2036f8a9a"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of verb forms within a sentence. A main clause requires a finite (tensed) verb to perform the action of the subject (in this case, Nery and her colleagues), and this choice supplies the finite past tense verb "published" to indicate that these biologists shared their findings about changes in whale genes associated with body size.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The nonfinite participle "publishing" doesn&rsquo;t supply the main clause with a finite verb. Choice C is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The nonfinite participle "having published" doesn&rsquo;t supply the main clause with a finite verb. Choice D is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The nonfinite to-infinitive "to publish" doesn&rsquo;t supply the main clause with a finite verb.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"cd4b1a7e","external_id":"aca54523-e3d5-4486-aac0-0b3b70e3ecbe","vaultid":null,"uId":"73cdbe43-fa65-4782-a824-1cf913ea6d0b","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>How did whales, once no bigger than seals, evolve to become the largest animals on Earth? Brazilian biologist Mariana Nery believes the answer might be found in whales&rsquo; DNA. In January 2023, Nery and her colleagues <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> a study showing changes over time in four whale genes associated with body size.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"664e7eb6-c566-45e9-affb-6ca2036f8a9a","content":"<p>published</p>"},{"id":"740e8d59-795d-4dcd-9bed-26ff4679c9c5","content":"<p>publishing</p>"},{"id":"641acfeb-420f-4320-9885-302818a1b88f","content":"<p>having published</p>"},{"id":"c917b569-b893-4649-b2d4-9d113a008396","content":"<p>to publish</p>"}],"keys":["664e7eb6-c566-45e9-affb-6ca2036f8a9a"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of verb forms within a sentence. A main clause requires a finite (tensed) verb to perform the action of the subject (in this case, Nery and her colleagues), and this choice supplies the finite past tense verb \"published\" to indicate that these biologists shared their findings about changes in whale genes associated with body size.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The nonfinite participle \"publishing\" doesn&rsquo;t supply the main clause with a finite verb. Choice C is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The nonfinite participle \"having published\" doesn&rsquo;t supply the main clause with a finite verb. Choice D is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The nonfinite to-infinitive \"to publish\" doesn&rsquo;t supply the main clause with a finite verb.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323049,"updateDate":1730147323049}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323049, 1730147323049),
    ($SATQ$cdbbbf94$SATQ$, $SATQ$7730a558-40d3-432a-8bcd-518885d552d6$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$e0edbd05-a5b7-4317-adc7-5092c2756837$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>As British scientist Peter Whibberley has observed, &ldquo;the Earth is not a very good timekeeper.&rdquo; Earth&rsquo;s slightly irregular rotation rate means that measurements of time must be periodically adjusted. Specifically, an extra &ldquo;leap second&rdquo; (the 86,401st second of the day) is <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> time based on the planet&rsquo;s rotation lags a full nine-tenths of a second behind time kept by precise atomic clocks.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"83f5c8cb-62a5-4373-9367-eee76d40f423","content":"<p>added, whenever</p>"},{"id":"3f5af28d-5cc5-4dec-98ef-a6e7a038ff5a","content":"<p>added; whenever</p>"},{"id":"7392333e-5469-451d-95c0-2548b8840493","content":"<p>added. Whenever</p>"},{"id":"b7950907-23f7-4c31-be15-bce27e1f83aa","content":"<p>added whenever</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["b7950907-23f7-4c31-be15-bce27e1f83aa"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation between a verb and a preposition. When, as in this case, a verb (&ldquo;is added&rdquo;) is immediately followed by a preposition (&ldquo;whenever&rdquo;), no punctuation is needed. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the verb and the preposition. Choice B is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the verb and the preposition. Choice C is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the verb and the preposition. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-706-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$f0807e80-7953-4f9a-973d-a30c061585f9$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"cdbbbf94","external_id":"7730a558-40d3-432a-8bcd-518885d552d6","vaultid":"e0edbd05-a5b7-4317-adc7-5092c2756837","uId":"3d59a1a1-4fae-42fa-b0aa-54b505a6b604","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>As British scientist Peter Whibberley has observed, &ldquo;the Earth is not a very good timekeeper.&rdquo; Earth&rsquo;s slightly irregular rotation rate means that measurements of time must be periodically adjusted. Specifically, an extra &ldquo;leap second&rdquo; (the 86,401st second of the day) is <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> time based on the planet&rsquo;s rotation lags a full nine-tenths of a second behind time kept by precise atomic clocks.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"83f5c8cb-62a5-4373-9367-eee76d40f423","content":"<p>added, whenever</p>"},{"id":"3f5af28d-5cc5-4dec-98ef-a6e7a038ff5a","content":"<p>added; whenever</p>"},{"id":"7392333e-5469-451d-95c0-2548b8840493","content":"<p>added. Whenever</p>"},{"id":"b7950907-23f7-4c31-be15-bce27e1f83aa","content":"<p>added whenever</p>"}],"keys":["b7950907-23f7-4c31-be15-bce27e1f83aa"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation between a verb and a preposition. When, as in this case, a verb (&ldquo;is added&rdquo;) is immediately followed by a preposition (&ldquo;whenever&rdquo;), no punctuation is needed. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the verb and the preposition. Choice B is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the verb and the preposition. Choice C is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the verb and the preposition. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-706-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"f0807e80-7953-4f9a-973d-a30c061585f9","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959347,"updateDate":1691007959347}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959347, 1691007959347),
    ($SATQ$ce81d0b7$SATQ$, $SATQ$45e3d22e-6b87-4603-b370-be6e4b42dcba$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The life spans of rockfish vary greatly by species. For instance, the colorful calico rockfish (<em>Sebastes dallii</em>) can survive for a little over a <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> the rougheye rockfish (<em>Sebastes aleutianus</em>) boasts a maximum life span of about two centuries.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"8eb2200c-c187-41ea-b467-35aeef54e308","content":"<p>decade<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">:</span> while</p>"},{"id":"ac0f4405-1c4c-43ca-a1a2-5bdd1402a718","content":"<p>decade<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">.</span> While</p>"},{"id":"cdb12309-5fea-41da-9e23-eb2be5b825c9","content":"<p>decade<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">;</span> while</p>"},{"id":"522f3a0a-536f-488f-b1a9-fa7b5872ade2","content":"<p>decade<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> while</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["522f3a0a-536f-488f-b1a9-fa7b5872ade2"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation between a main clause and a subordinate clause. This choice correctly uses a comma to mark the boundary between the main clause ("the colorful&hellip;decade") and the subordinate clause ("while&hellip;centuries") that provides contrasting information about the life span of rougheye rockfish.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because a colon can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join a main clause and a subordinate clause. Choice B is incorrect because it results in a rhetorically unacceptable sentence fragment beginning with "while." Choice C is incorrect because a semicolon can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join a main clause and a subordinate clause.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"ce81d0b7","external_id":"45e3d22e-6b87-4603-b370-be6e4b42dcba","vaultid":null,"uId":"6ea0a736-0b4d-4461-a55d-016b1f5fd721","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The life spans of rockfish vary greatly by species. For instance, the colorful calico rockfish (<em>Sebastes dallii</em>) can survive for a little over a <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> the rougheye rockfish (<em>Sebastes aleutianus</em>) boasts a maximum life span of about two centuries.&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"8eb2200c-c187-41ea-b467-35aeef54e308","content":"<p>decade<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">:</span> while</p>"},{"id":"ac0f4405-1c4c-43ca-a1a2-5bdd1402a718","content":"<p>decade<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">.</span> While</p>"},{"id":"cdb12309-5fea-41da-9e23-eb2be5b825c9","content":"<p>decade<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">;</span> while</p>"},{"id":"522f3a0a-536f-488f-b1a9-fa7b5872ade2","content":"<p>decade<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> while</p>"}],"keys":["522f3a0a-536f-488f-b1a9-fa7b5872ade2"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation between a main clause and a subordinate clause. This choice correctly uses a comma to mark the boundary between the main clause (\"the colorful&hellip;decade\") and the subordinate clause (\"while&hellip;centuries\") that provides contrasting information about the life span of rougheye rockfish.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because a colon can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join a main clause and a subordinate clause. Choice B is incorrect because it results in a rhetorically unacceptable sentence fragment beginning with \"while.\" Choice C is incorrect because a semicolon can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join a main clause and a subordinate clause.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1691007959346,"updateDate":1691007959346}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959346, 1691007959346),
    ($SATQ$cf08e2fd$SATQ$, $SATQ$6939cd22-4028-4cd4-8c6c-715efed88077$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>From 1912 to 1951, Charlotta Bass owned and operated the newspaper <em>The California Eagle</em>. While it was under Bass&rsquo;s leadership, <em>The Eagle</em> <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> one of the US&rsquo;s most influential Black-owned newspapers.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"250374a4-7214-4b01-b949-b43f76ce38de","content":"<p>will become</p>"},{"id":"b3121943-96f2-4982-9382-78236bff59db","content":"<p>became</p>"},{"id":"956cc184-da36-4c0b-93ec-951d00d751f3","content":"<p>is becoming</p>"},{"id":"db79fcf4-fc4b-45bd-bc03-60779f10aed5","content":"<p>to become</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["b3121943-96f2-4982-9382-78236bff59db"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The conventions being tested are the use of verbs to express tense and the use of verb forms within a sentence. In this choice, the past tense verb "became" is consistent with the other past tense verbs ("owned," "operated," and "was") used to indicate that, at a period of time in the past, Bass&rsquo;s leadership resulted in her newspaper becoming one of the most influential Black-owned newspapers in the US. In addition, "became" supplies the main clause with a finite (tensed) verb, which is required to perform the action of the subject (in this case, "<em>The Eagle</em>").</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the future tense verb "will become" isn&rsquo;t consistent with the other past tense verbs used to discuss Bass and her newspaper. Choice C is incorrect because the present progressive verb "is becoming" isn&rsquo;t consistent with the other past tense verbs used to discuss Bass and her newspaper. Choice D is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The nonfinite to-infinitive "to become" doesn&rsquo;t supply the main clause with a finite verb.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"cf08e2fd","external_id":"6939cd22-4028-4cd4-8c6c-715efed88077","vaultid":null,"uId":"e0d7543b-2a23-4535-bb3a-23d3c2976bbd","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>From 1912 to 1951, Charlotta Bass owned and operated the newspaper <em>The California Eagle</em>. While it was under Bass&rsquo;s leadership, <em>The Eagle</em> <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> one of the US&rsquo;s most influential Black-owned newspapers.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"250374a4-7214-4b01-b949-b43f76ce38de","content":"<p>will become</p>"},{"id":"b3121943-96f2-4982-9382-78236bff59db","content":"<p>became</p>"},{"id":"956cc184-da36-4c0b-93ec-951d00d751f3","content":"<p>is becoming</p>"},{"id":"db79fcf4-fc4b-45bd-bc03-60779f10aed5","content":"<p>to become</p>"}],"keys":["b3121943-96f2-4982-9382-78236bff59db"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The conventions being tested are the use of verbs to express tense and the use of verb forms within a sentence. In this choice, the past tense verb \"became\" is consistent with the other past tense verbs (\"owned,\" \"operated,\" and \"was\") used to indicate that, at a period of time in the past, Bass&rsquo;s leadership resulted in her newspaper becoming one of the most influential Black-owned newspapers in the US. In addition, \"became\" supplies the main clause with a finite (tensed) verb, which is required to perform the action of the subject (in this case, \"<em>The Eagle</em>\").</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the future tense verb \"will become\" isn&rsquo;t consistent with the other past tense verbs used to discuss Bass and her newspaper. Choice C is incorrect because the present progressive verb \"is becoming\" isn&rsquo;t consistent with the other past tense verbs used to discuss Bass and her newspaper. Choice D is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The nonfinite to-infinitive \"to become\" doesn&rsquo;t supply the main clause with a finite verb.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323050,"updateDate":1730147323050}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323050, 1730147323050),
    ($SATQ$cfe23776$SATQ$, $SATQ$5aeac962-fa14-4491-8991-56ff0ec4b5c6$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>According to the original text of the US Constitution, written in <span data-ssml-say-as="date">1787</span>, the presidential candidate receiving the second-most Electoral College votes becomes vice president. The 12th amendment, ratified in <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> separated the elections for the two offices.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a8cb3434-f222-4e42-9cea-c063aad64856","content":"<p><span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">1804</span><span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">&mdash;</span></p>"},{"id":"e3cb1030-bc4e-4ff6-b1a3-7e205e758e25","content":"<p><span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">1804</span><span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span></p>"},{"id":"7ba43701-b8ee-4a1c-b461-c8fdbb5bb652","content":"<p><span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">1804</span><span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">:</span></p>"},{"id":"22aacebf-7ddb-4a49-9b51-29d5e6b28066","content":"<p><span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">1804</span></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["e3cb1030-bc4e-4ff6-b1a3-7e205e758e25"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of punctuation within a sentence. The comma after &ldquo;1804&rdquo; pairs with the comma after &ldquo;amendment&rdquo; to separate the supplementary element &ldquo;ratified in 1804&rdquo; from the rest of the sentence. This supplementary element functions to provide additional information on the term &ldquo;12th amendment,&rdquo; and the pair of commas indicates that this element could be removed without affecting the grammatical coherence of the sentence.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because a dash can&rsquo;t be paired with a comma in this way to separate the supplementary element from the rest of the sentence. Choice C is incorrect because a colon can&rsquo;t be paired with a comma in this way to separate the supplementary element from the rest of the sentence. Choice D is incorrect because it fails to use appropriate punctuation to separate the supplemental element from the rest of the sentence.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"cfe23776","external_id":"5aeac962-fa14-4491-8991-56ff0ec4b5c6","vaultid":null,"uId":"5fa0e535-f100-43ac-9ae5-a4ffac16b7c0","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>According to the original text of the US Constitution, written in <span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">1787</span>, the presidential candidate receiving the second-most Electoral College votes becomes vice president. The 12th amendment, ratified in <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> separated the elections for the two offices.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"a8cb3434-f222-4e42-9cea-c063aad64856","content":"<p><span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">1804</span><span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">&mdash;</span></p>"},{"id":"e3cb1030-bc4e-4ff6-b1a3-7e205e758e25","content":"<p><span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">1804</span><span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span></p>"},{"id":"7ba43701-b8ee-4a1c-b461-c8fdbb5bb652","content":"<p><span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">1804</span><span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">:</span></p>"},{"id":"22aacebf-7ddb-4a49-9b51-29d5e6b28066","content":"<p><span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">1804</span></p>"}],"keys":["e3cb1030-bc4e-4ff6-b1a3-7e205e758e25"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of punctuation within a sentence. The comma after &ldquo;1804&rdquo; pairs with the comma after &ldquo;amendment&rdquo; to separate the supplementary element &ldquo;ratified in 1804&rdquo; from the rest of the sentence. This supplementary element functions to provide additional information on the term &ldquo;12th amendment,&rdquo; and the pair of commas indicates that this element could be removed without affecting the grammatical coherence of the sentence.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because a dash can&rsquo;t be paired with a comma in this way to separate the supplementary element from the rest of the sentence. Choice C is incorrect because a colon can&rsquo;t be paired with a comma in this way to separate the supplementary element from the rest of the sentence. Choice D is incorrect because it fails to use appropriate punctuation to separate the supplemental element from the rest of the sentence.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1776362771168,"updateDate":1776362771168}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362771168, 1776362771168),
    ($SATQ$d073983d$SATQ$, $SATQ$aafb7917-da63-4f45-a9cf-94812aae98f2$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$beed77f4-031e-4e52-ba37-bb92d32bcd59$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Known for her massive photorealistic paintings of African American figures floating or swimming in pools, Calida Garcia <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> was the logical choice to design the book cover for Ta-Nehisi Coates&rsquo;s <em>The Water Dancer,</em> a novel about an African American man who can travel great distances through water.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"d3c3f7fe-4c0e-48f7-b277-64ecb1a390a3","content":"<p>Rawles&mdash;</p>"},{"id":"2cb6d580-7eba-4263-8120-3a1e39c93d03","content":"<p>Rawles:</p>"},{"id":"8794758c-b035-4eae-9c93-2313f8b7d665","content":"<p>Rawles,</p>"},{"id":"aca4b7d8-f68a-49f8-a39c-81c3fc63b501","content":"<p>Rawles</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["aca4b7d8-f68a-49f8-a39c-81c3fc63b501"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation between a subject and a verb. When, as in this case, a subject (&ldquo;Calida Garcia Rawles&rdquo;) is immediately followed by a verb (&ldquo;was&rdquo;), no punctuation is needed. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the subject and the verb. Choice B is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the subject and the verb. Choice C is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the subject and the verb. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-617-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$76a497dc-e94c-4d86-af6b-a16097777466$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL