ed between the integrated relative clause beginning with &ldquo;that often&rdquo; and the noun phrase that it modifies (&ldquo;graffiti-like scribbles&rdquo;). Choice D is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the integrated relative clause beginning with &ldquo;that often&rdquo; and the noun phrase that it modifies (&ldquo;graffiti-like scribbles&rdquo;).</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"d198997b","external_id":"7242e88d-e335-4dc4-b9df-662c2488fc9b","vaultid":null,"uId":"577965ef-9acc-4397-aed0-c3d92583da86","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Cy Twombly, a US painter and sculptor, created many large-scale abstract works, such as his 10-painting series <em>Fifty Days at Iliam</em>. In these works, Twombly&rsquo;s artistic style is exemplified by his use of graffiti-like <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> often incorporate words or phrases from poetry and mythology.&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"c97a2755-83e7-488c-b80b-d3fb6e3afd77","content":"<p>scribbles<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">:</span> that</p>"},{"id":"88e04abf-8370-4d02-b76d-3259d681d561","content":"<p>scribbles that</p>"},{"id":"d6596f7d-88ff-4a26-8d49-7e50df208788","content":"<p>scribbles<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">;</span> that</p>"},{"id":"32ad2b87-d3a1-492d-8ba6-598d92942da0","content":"<p>scribbles<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">.</span> That</p>"}],"keys":["88e04abf-8370-4d02-b76d-3259d681d561"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use and punctuation of a relative clause. This choice correctly uses the relative pronoun &ldquo;that&rdquo; and no punctuation to create an integrated relative clause that provides essential information about the noun phrase (&ldquo;graffiti-like scribbles&rdquo;) that it modifies.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the integrated relative clause beginning with &ldquo;that often&rdquo; and the noun phrase that it modifies (&ldquo;graffiti-like scribbles&rdquo;). Choice C is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the integrated relative clause beginning with &ldquo;that often&rdquo; and the noun phrase that it modifies (&ldquo;graffiti-like scribbles&rdquo;). Choice D is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the integrated relative clause beginning with &ldquo;that often&rdquo; and the noun phrase that it modifies (&ldquo;graffiti-like scribbles&rdquo;).</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1776362771169,"updateDate":1776362771169}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362771169, 1776362771169),
    ($SATQ$d222f7d9$SATQ$, $SATQ$928ce2c4-f5f3-415f-943e-0728649989ed$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Helical <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> widely understood to confer stability and efficiency in the locomotion of a variety of microscopic organisms&mdash;including bacteria, eukaryotic algae, and ciliates&mdash;bestows similar advantages, albeit via different propulsive modes, to larger oceanic macroplanktons, such as salps.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"10503124-acda-4859-99ef-2e4632877d95","content":"<p>swimming<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> is</p>"},{"id":"503967f3-f7c8-497b-8f82-7527e090bac3","content":"<p>swimming is</p>"},{"id":"cb292c78-d504-4434-9131-6f3b53132a93","content":"<p>swimming has been</p>"},{"id":"0f6144b0-53c7-49b3-bc7e-457d59ae0ffd","content":"<p>swimming<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["0f6144b0-53c7-49b3-bc7e-457d59ae0ffd"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of punctuation within a sentence. The comma after &ldquo;swimming&rdquo; pairs with the dash after &ldquo;ciliates&rdquo; to offset the supplementary element (&ldquo;widely&hellip;ciliates&rdquo;) that interrupts the main clause of the sentence.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Adding the verb &ldquo;is&rdquo; here creates an ungrammatical sentence structure with two main verbs (&ldquo;is&rdquo; and &ldquo;bestows&rdquo;) without appropriate punctuation and/or conjunction. Choice B is incorrect because it fails to offset the supplementary element with appropriate punctuation. Furthermore, adding the verb &ldquo;is&rdquo; here creates an ungrammatical sentence that has two finite verbs (&ldquo;is&rdquo; and &ldquo;bestows&rdquo;) without appropriate punctuation and/or conjunction. Choice C is incorrect because it fails to offset the supplementary element with appropriate punctuation. Furthermore, adding the verb &ldquo;has been&rdquo; here creates an ungrammatical sentence that has two finite verbs (&ldquo;has been&rdquo; and &ldquo;bestows&rdquo;) without appropriate punctuation and/or conjunction.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"d222f7d9","external_id":"928ce2c4-f5f3-415f-943e-0728649989ed","vaultid":null,"uId":"f704f893-a212-4e33-b290-f8050192599a","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Helical <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> widely understood to confer stability and efficiency in the locomotion of a variety of microscopic organisms&mdash;including bacteria, eukaryotic algae, and ciliates&mdash;bestows similar advantages, albeit via different propulsive modes, to larger oceanic macroplanktons, such as salps.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"10503124-acda-4859-99ef-2e4632877d95","content":"<p>swimming<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> is</p>"},{"id":"503967f3-f7c8-497b-8f82-7527e090bac3","content":"<p>swimming is</p>"},{"id":"cb292c78-d504-4434-9131-6f3b53132a93","content":"<p>swimming has been</p>"},{"id":"0f6144b0-53c7-49b3-bc7e-457d59ae0ffd","content":"<p>swimming<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span></p>"}],"keys":["0f6144b0-53c7-49b3-bc7e-457d59ae0ffd"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of punctuation within a sentence. The comma after &ldquo;swimming&rdquo; pairs with the dash after &ldquo;ciliates&rdquo; to offset the supplementary element (&ldquo;widely&hellip;ciliates&rdquo;) that interrupts the main clause of the sentence.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Adding the verb &ldquo;is&rdquo; here creates an ungrammatical sentence structure with two main verbs (&ldquo;is&rdquo; and &ldquo;bestows&rdquo;) without appropriate punctuation and/or conjunction. Choice B is incorrect because it fails to offset the supplementary element with appropriate punctuation. Furthermore, adding the verb &ldquo;is&rdquo; here creates an ungrammatical sentence that has two finite verbs (&ldquo;is&rdquo; and &ldquo;bestows&rdquo;) without appropriate punctuation and/or conjunction. Choice C is incorrect because it fails to offset the supplementary element with appropriate punctuation. Furthermore, adding the verb &ldquo;has been&rdquo; here creates an ungrammatical sentence that has two finite verbs (&ldquo;has been&rdquo; and &ldquo;bestows&rdquo;) without appropriate punctuation and/or conjunction.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1776362771169,"updateDate":1776362771169}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362771169, 1776362771169),
    ($SATQ$d2b81427$SATQ$, $SATQ$2ecd025a-d554-43f3-81bd-70826491a1b8$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$af09f4c3-aded-40c2-b89d-7d4d22b2f4a6$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In assessing the films of Japanese director Akira Kurosawa, <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> have missed his equally deep engagement with Japanese artistic traditions such as Noh theater.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a567d491-9b10-4942-90ee-9fc416aa64fc","content":"<p>many critics have focused on Kurosawa&rsquo;s use of Western literary sources but&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"a48119a7-e7f6-4d00-848a-04659d4881e0","content":"<p>Kurosawa&rsquo;s use of Western literary sources has been the focus of many critics, who</p>"},{"id":"335c5e1c-90fd-4703-9af3-05fce3c7c2b8","content":"<p>there are many critics who have focused on Kurosawa&rsquo;s use of Western literary sources, but they</p>"},{"id":"7dd0bb53-5569-4a88-b756-5dbdbc838e18","content":"<p>the focus of many critics has been on Kurosawa&rsquo;s use of Western literary sources; they</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["a567d491-9b10-4942-90ee-9fc416aa64fc"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-modifier placement. This choice makes the noun phrase &ldquo;many critics&rdquo; the subject of the sentence and places it immediately after the modifying phrase &ldquo;in assessing&hellip;Kurosawa.&rdquo; In doing so, this choice clearly establishes that it is the critics&mdash;and not another noun in the sentence&mdash;who assess Kurosawa&rsquo;s films. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun phrase &ldquo;Kurosawa&rsquo;s&hellip;sources&rdquo; immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that his use of Western literary sources is what assesses Kurosawa&rsquo;s films. Choice C is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the function word &ldquo;there&rdquo; immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that &ldquo;there&rdquo; is what assesses Kurosawa&rsquo;s films. Choice D is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun phrase &ldquo;the focus&hellip;critics&rdquo; immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that the critics&rsquo; focus is what assesses Kurosawa&rsquo;s films. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-654-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$bcf74803-108e-498b-a5e2-8a8c17549908$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"d2b81427","external_id":"2ecd025a-d554-43f3-81bd-70826491a1b8","vaultid":"af09f4c3-aded-40c2-b89d-7d4d22b2f4a6","uId":"3f18edb2-74cf-4af6-b357-749944ae5eaf","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In assessing the films of Japanese director Akira Kurosawa, <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> have missed his equally deep engagement with Japanese artistic traditions such as Noh theater.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"a567d491-9b10-4942-90ee-9fc416aa64fc","content":"<p>many critics have focused on Kurosawa&rsquo;s use of Western literary sources but&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"a48119a7-e7f6-4d00-848a-04659d4881e0","content":"<p>Kurosawa&rsquo;s use of Western literary sources has been the focus of many critics, who</p>"},{"id":"335c5e1c-90fd-4703-9af3-05fce3c7c2b8","content":"<p>there are many critics who have focused on Kurosawa&rsquo;s use of Western literary sources, but they</p>"},{"id":"7dd0bb53-5569-4a88-b756-5dbdbc838e18","content":"<p>the focus of many critics has been on Kurosawa&rsquo;s use of Western literary sources; they</p>"}],"keys":["a567d491-9b10-4942-90ee-9fc416aa64fc"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-modifier placement. This choice makes the noun phrase &ldquo;many critics&rdquo; the subject of the sentence and places it immediately after the modifying phrase &ldquo;in assessing&hellip;Kurosawa.&rdquo; In doing so, this choice clearly establishes that it is the critics&mdash;and not another noun in the sentence&mdash;who assess Kurosawa&rsquo;s films. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun phrase &ldquo;Kurosawa&rsquo;s&hellip;sources&rdquo; immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that his use of Western literary sources is what assesses Kurosawa&rsquo;s films. Choice C is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the function word &ldquo;there&rdquo; immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that &ldquo;there&rdquo; is what assesses Kurosawa&rsquo;s films. Choice D is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun phrase &ldquo;the focus&hellip;critics&rdquo; immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that the critics&rsquo; focus is what assesses Kurosawa&rsquo;s films. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-654-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"bcf74803-108e-498b-a5e2-8a8c17549908","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959341,"updateDate":1691007959341}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959341, 1691007959341),
    ($SATQ$d2cf0e11$SATQ$, $SATQ$1cda4e01-b0b3-4d26-9863-488d98878381$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$d67b8783-a106-479f-aa65-89faf46d0524$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Inventor John Friedman created a prototype of the first flexible straw by inserting a screw into a paper straw and, using dental floss, binding the straw tightly around the <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> When the floss and screw were removed, the resulting corrugations in the paper allowed the straw to bend easily over the edge of a glass. </p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"ec62a565-f62b-4136-9111-57073ba1b255","content":"<p>screw&rsquo;s thread&rsquo;s.</p>"},{"id":"17b33b8c-2cdc-44f9-b1be-d1c3f20bd949","content":"<p>screws&rsquo; threads.</p>"},{"id":"ed4d1717-750e-44a6-a379-ec40abfeda65","content":"<p>screw&rsquo;s threads.</p>"},{"id":"cc3623e9-5d89-4a12-883d-fefa49d18969","content":"<p>screws threads&rsquo;.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["ed4d1717-750e-44a6-a379-ec40abfeda65"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of plural and possessive nouns. The singular possessive noun &ldquo;screw&rsquo;s&rdquo; and the plural noun &ldquo;threads&rdquo; correctly indicate that there is only one screw and it has multiple threads. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the context requires the plural noun &ldquo;threads,&rdquo; not the singular possessive noun &ldquo;thread&rsquo;s.&rdquo; Choice B is incorrect because the context requires the singular possessive noun &ldquo;screw&rsquo;s,&rdquo; not the plural possessive noun &ldquo;screws&rsquo;.&rdquo; Choice D is incorrect because the context requires the singular possessive noun &ldquo;screw&rsquo;s&rdquo; and the plural noun &ldquo;threads,&rdquo; not the plural noun &ldquo;screws&rdquo; or the plural possessive noun &ldquo;threads.&rsquo;&rdquo; </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-648-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$8883972b-03d9-4277-adff-9a4bf5a16b01$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"d2cf0e11","external_id":"1cda4e01-b0b3-4d26-9863-488d98878381","vaultid":"d67b8783-a106-479f-aa65-89faf46d0524","uId":"bcdd9511-13bc-4707-a0a9-e82185d1c775","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Inventor John Friedman created a prototype of the first flexible straw by inserting a screw into a paper straw and, using dental floss, binding the straw tightly around the <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> When the floss and screw were removed, the resulting corrugations in the paper allowed the straw to bend easily over the edge of a glass. </p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"ec62a565-f62b-4136-9111-57073ba1b255","content":"<p>screw&rsquo;s thread&rsquo;s.</p>"},{"id":"17b33b8c-2cdc-44f9-b1be-d1c3f20bd949","content":"<p>screws&rsquo; threads.</p>"},{"id":"ed4d1717-750e-44a6-a379-ec40abfeda65","content":"<p>screw&rsquo;s threads.</p>"},{"id":"cc3623e9-5d89-4a12-883d-fefa49d18969","content":"<p>screws threads&rsquo;.</p>"}],"keys":["ed4d1717-750e-44a6-a379-ec40abfeda65"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of plural and possessive nouns. The singular possessive noun &ldquo;screw&rsquo;s&rdquo; and the plural noun &ldquo;threads&rdquo; correctly indicate that there is only one screw and it has multiple threads. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the context requires the plural noun &ldquo;threads,&rdquo; not the singular possessive noun &ldquo;thread&rsquo;s.&rdquo; Choice B is incorrect because the context requires the singular possessive noun &ldquo;screw&rsquo;s,&rdquo; not the plural possessive noun &ldquo;screws&rsquo;.&rdquo; Choice D is incorrect because the context requires the singular possessive noun &ldquo;screw&rsquo;s&rdquo; and the plural noun &ldquo;threads,&rdquo; not the plural noun &ldquo;screws&rdquo; or the plural possessive noun &ldquo;threads.&rsquo;&rdquo; </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-648-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"8883972b-03d9-4277-adff-9a4bf5a16b01","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959340,"updateDate":1691007959340}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959340, 1691007959340),
    ($SATQ$d46ac7e7$SATQ$, $SATQ$12e5c35a-4f83-4ddc-a24e-2561f79aa6ff$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$5c701cba-7570-4e91-a001-acad19d4bb87$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>A second-generation Japanese American, Wataru Misaka <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> in World War II (1941-45) and won two amateur national basketball championships at the University of Utah when he joined the New York Knicks for the 1947-48 season, becoming the first non-white basketball player in the US&rsquo;s top professional league.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"5691ac33-835d-4ad3-9be7-360ad9b7d4da","content":"<p>already served</p>"},{"id":"547838cc-0ea6-495a-8d31-aaa123f4c31b","content":"<p>was already serving</p>"},{"id":"10071597-e8d9-460a-b6ca-77179ee4f4da","content":"<p>already serves</p>"},{"id":"59ca464c-336f-4129-8c55-19d9d081addf","content":"<p>had already served</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["59ca464c-336f-4129-8c55-19d9d081addf"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. Misaka served in World War II before he joined the New York Knicks in 1947. To show that a past occurrence took place before another past occurrence, we need to use &ldquo;had&rdquo; + the past tense form of the verb. This is called the past perfect tense. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Misaka served in World War II before he joined the Knicks. Both events are in the past, but his service in World War II happened earlier, so we need a verb that makes it clear that his service (and the two national championships) had ended by the time he joined the Knicks. Choice B is incorrect. &ldquo;Was already serving&rdquo; forms the continuous past tense, which we use when we&rsquo;re showing a past action that was ongoing. Misaka served in World War II before he joined the Knicks. Both events are in the past, but they&rsquo;re not happening at the same time, so we shouldn&rsquo;t use the continuous past tense here. Choice C is incorrect. Misaka served in World War II in the past, so we shouldn&rsquo;t use the present tense &ldquo;serves.&rdquo; </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-FSS-H007$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$58bfe021-8ce5-40de-8c76-316296f9c929$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"d46ac7e7","external_id":"12e5c35a-4f83-4ddc-a24e-2561f79aa6ff","vaultid":"5c701cba-7570-4e91-a001-acad19d4bb87","uId":"b52c6e36-15cd-4a2b-a132-fa0176d3521f","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>A second-generation Japanese American, Wataru Misaka <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> in World War II (1941-45) and won two amateur national basketball championships at the University of Utah when he joined the New York Knicks for the 1947-48 season, becoming the first non-white basketball player in the US&rsquo;s top professional league.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"5691ac33-835d-4ad3-9be7-360ad9b7d4da","content":"<p>already served</p>"},{"id":"547838cc-0ea6-495a-8d31-aaa123f4c31b","content":"<p>was already serving</p>"},{"id":"10071597-e8d9-460a-b6ca-77179ee4f4da","content":"<p>already serves</p>"},{"id":"59ca464c-336f-4129-8c55-19d9d081addf","content":"<p>had already served</p>"}],"keys":["59ca464c-336f-4129-8c55-19d9d081addf"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. Misaka served in World War II before he joined the New York Knicks in 1947. To show that a past occurrence took place before another past occurrence, we need to use &ldquo;had&rdquo; + the past tense form of the verb. This is called the past perfect tense. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Misaka served in World War II before he joined the Knicks. Both events are in the past, but his service in World War II happened earlier, so we need a verb that makes it clear that his service (and the two national championships) had ended by the time he joined the Knicks. Choice B is incorrect. &ldquo;Was already serving&rdquo; forms the continuous past tense, which we use when we&rsquo;re showing a past action that was ongoing. Misaka served in World War II before he joined the Knicks. Both events are in the past, but they&rsquo;re not happening at the same time, so we shouldn&rsquo;t use the continuous past tense here. Choice C is incorrect. Misaka served in World War II in the past, so we shouldn&rsquo;t use the present tense &ldquo;serves.&rdquo; </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-FSS-H007","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"58bfe021-8ce5-40de-8c76-316296f9c929","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959367,"updateDate":1691007959367}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959367, 1691007959367)
